Wire standalone A-MSDU TX into the rtl8822cu USB xmit path (v4).

Realtek only wired rtw_xmitframe_coalesce_amsdu() into the PCIe xmit path; on USB
it is dead code with no caller, so the `tx_amsdu` proc knob is inert.

This is the only aggregation available on this link. The peer is a userspace libusb
station that cannot emit a compressed BlockAck, so the AP's per-STA
agg_enable_bitmap stays 0 permanently (80+ issue_addba_req, never established) and
MAC efficiency sits near 40% of PHY. A-MSDU needs no BlockAck.

Three previous attempts each regressed, all on the same theme -- manipulating the
xmit queue by hand:

  v1  Hand-rolled the "cannot pair" send: rtw_dump_xframe() on a frame that never
      went through rtw_xmitframe_coalesce(), so nr_frags/last_txcmdsz and the txdesc
      were unset. rtw_dump_xframe() submits its URB *inside*
      `for (t = 0; t < nr_frags; t++)`, so nothing was written and the xmit_buf was
      never returned by the TX-complete callback -> leak -> pool exhaustion -> 137x
      "xmit_status_check tx hang", STAs stopped associating.

  v2  Fixed that leak (0 hangs) and achieved real pairing -- the receiver saw
      A-MSDUs with exactly 2 subframes each -- but still hand-rolled the single
      send, which SILENTLY dropped ~92% of frames (4343 dequeued vs ~355
      transmitted per 10s, tx_dropped=0, nothing in dmesg): 80% RTP loss, black
      screen. It also returned _FALSE to implement a 1 ms hold timer, but the caller
      does `if (ret == _FALSE) break;`, which breaks the whole TX drain loop.
      Toggling tx_amsdu alone on an otherwise identical module measured 34 Mbps off
      vs 0.3-0.8 Mbps on.

  v3  Removed both, and returned unpairable frames with rtw_xmitframe_enqueue().
      That appends at the TAIL, so an EAPOL frame could be sequenced behind a
      re-enqueued data frame and the station stopped completing the 4-way handshake.

v4 removes the last piece of hand-rolled queue manipulation. New
rtw_dequeue_xframe_amsdu_pair() decides and removes atomically under
pxmitpriv->lock: it either yields two aggregatable frames or leaves the queue
completely untouched, so frame order is never disturbed and control-plane traffic
is never displaced. When it yields nothing the pre-pass simply falls through to the
driver's own aggregation path.

Both frames come from the same per-STA/per-AC servq, so the RA matches by
construction and only the TID is compared (TIDs 0 and 3 both map to BE).
check_amsdu() already rejects EAPOL/ARP/DHCP/WEP/TKIP and multicast.

Also sets pkt_offset/agg_num before coalescing: coalesce_amsdu() derives
hw_hdr_offset = TXDESC_SIZE + pkt_offset*PACKET_OFFSET_SZ under
CONFIG_USB_TX_AGGREGATION, and update_txdesc() uses pkt_offset to decide whether to
pull the txdesc head. Both were zero in v1/v2.

Still pairs only (2 subframes). The dongle advertises a 7935-byte maximum A-MSDU
length (HT cap) and 11454-byte maximum MPDU (VHT cap), so up to ~6 subframes would
fit; extending the builder to N is the next step once this is proven stable.

--- a/core/rtw_xmit.c
+++ b/core/rtw_xmit.c
@@ -4346,6 +4346,101 @@
 }
